ed in the  lives of our subjects and the idea of  biographical stance is one of mild  affection love is dangerous hatred is  poisonous but some affection mild  affection should be there otherwise it's  not much fun to spend seven or fourteen  years in the company of this person also  going through the letters of  interviewing the children of studying  the the life of listening to the  recordings of this person one becomes  almost a participant in the life I  happen to be a participant in Reagan's  life in the sense that I was able to go  to the White House during his presidency  and be with him and interview him and  observe him and follow him around and I  developed a closeness of POV of  the point of view that was so close that  I began to feel the absence of that  closeness when I went back to earlier  parts of his life when I wasn't actually  there so I decided to do to make  explicit what is implicit in all  biographies and  is put my self in the form of a  fictitious observer into Reagan's early  life a person of whom he was always  unaware was Reagan tended to be of  everybody and preserved this closeness  of you all the way through it was a  technique that I realized would be  controversial and insert he did it  proved to be so but I do think that it  was an honest device and I'm kind of  encouraged to notice that other writers  have have have moved in similar  directions since for example Andrew  Motion who is the poet laureate of Great  Britain after Dutch was published came  out of the book on Thomas Wainwright the  Romantic poet who was involved in a  murder case and was transported to  Australia and remotion wrote this very  scholarly book as an autobiography it's  written in Wainwright's own voice and  the end of every chapter and remotion  gets into a debate with his narrator so  it's a post modernist technique that I  think is honest and exciting and I think  it'll be followed by more such  experiments yes you know Peter Ackroyd  and his biography of Dickens where he  emulates Dickens and converses with  Dickens and it's it's another way of  approaching the subject in some ways  enviable because you can just make it up  Christopher well I think it's um it's  not so necessary even to effect  neutrality for example at Thomas Carlyle  when he set out to do Cromwell was  self-consciously trying to do a rescue  operation on a reputation he said  dragging him out as he put it from  underneath a heap of dead dogs and  carrion in order to so dust him down and  dispel this terrible a cloud of slander  that had accumulated and I think in  Isaac deutsches wonderful three volume  iboga fee of leon trotsky he I'm fairly  sure he starts by saying he wants to do  for Trotsky what what Carlisle did for  Cromwell and as long as this is openly  admitted I think that's perfectly fine I  only know it from the opposite  standpoint because one of the reasons I  said no to your proposal about Thatcher  was that it had when young had a very  very brief and very unsatisfactory  sexual encounter with her but I wasn't  completely certain that this wouldn't  sub taint the whole thing but the other  time that I had to do a study of a  person there's a living one  it wasn't exactly a biography of did see  an extended political profile and and  inquiry into the life and indeed the  crimes of Henry Kissinger and by the  time I was finished with that I felt as  if I got gangrene I can tell you if you  if you sir just be get up in the morning  with Henry and then go to bed still  thinking about him over an extended  period it will make you sick and when  people come to me and ask me to sign the  book and say they want to read all they  have read I said what did it make you  feel ill that's my test for this book  have I passed on at least some of the  feeling of nausea and disgust and  usually yes I have managed to offload  some of it but it's written I must tell  you with a tone of perfect detachment  and neutrality you know it I didn't know  about that sir by the way that's  you know it occurs to me thinking about  our three biographers here that that  your subjects and by the way you've also  you wrote about Kissinger with distinct  antipathy but with great sympathy for  Orwell and Jefferson  two other subjects I have an answer now  to that the I used to get approached by  motherly ladies in bookstores when I did  signings and they would be worried and  they come up to me and say we know don't  you like anybody as if I was so secretly  unhappy and venting no no look I've  written always furry books about people  who I like you know I think is sweet  yeah but Christopher let's not forget  about Mother Teresa this is a guy who  did a teardown job on Mother Teresa  it's an easy target what else well I  said she's an easy target but you got I  didn't know this was gonna turn into a  sitcom here this morning feel like I  have Seinfeld up here but in any event  one of the things that interested me  about you're among the many things about  your biography of Jefferson was that you  have a great love affair with America  and have written many essays about that  and of course you you view this from the  perspective of an expatriate who has now  become an American citizen is that right  and so I always think that that there is  some very distinct vantage point of view  that enables one to as it were solve  problems solve issues Express issues in  your own life without being too  Freudian about it but but Francine's  subject is also very different from  anything you've written and Edmunds  emerges out of your your lifelong  passion for music it interests me that  you that you chose these subjects why  why is it what is the appeal of someone  who is so far in a way from your own  experience and yet answer is so deeply  two questions in your own life maybe you  could address that well it's true the  difference when one believes as I do  have come to believe that the American  Revolution is the only revolution left  