 deepened the whole of  slavery as well he made certain that it  went into the New Territories over the  objections of people like pain and  Barlow by the way not to get carried  away with this but the thing you'll  sometimes told is you mustn't judge by  contemporary standards biographer  mustn't impose as it were our prejudices  or our wisdoms on the past I often think  that some that's false friendship  an advice as piece of advice because  there were people like Joel Barlow and  Thomas Paine who thought exactly about  slavery as we like to think we do and  who tried to say to Jefferson don't do  this mr. president we we can begin again  with Louisiana without this terrible  system but by agreeing to do it he made  sure absolutely sure not just that there  would be a protracting of the  institution and a deepening an extension  of it he made certain there would be a  civil war about it and I hadn't really  appreciated that the situation had been  as grave as that now but it but he he  was a slave owner but he was not a  murderer  so unlike Caravaggio who you you had to  overlook this minor character in the  back of my mind or the front of my mind  the whole time I was writing about  Caravaggio it was the kind of the larger  abstract question of of why certain  works of art endure and and how certain  works of art continued to speak to us  over time and centuries so a particular  problem writing about Caravaggio is that  so little was known about his life I  mean he left no journals he left no  letters he left no preparatory drawings  and most of what we know about  Caravaggio in fact comes to us through  police reports court desert depositions  complaints of his neighbors and so forth  so so really all I could do or what I  mainly did was look at the paintings and  I thought the longer I look at these  paintings and the harder I think about  these paintings the more I'm going to  know about Caravaggio and in fact  there's a very kind of intimate  communication with the painter that that  does occur aided by the fact that he  that he put self-portraits into so many  of his paintings and when you look at at  you know the great famous David with the  head of Goliath which was one of his  final paintings and he's holding the  young David is holding the head of glass  and in fact it's a double self-portrait  it's the it's the young Caravaggio and  the the old wounded Caravaggio and if  you look at that  picture long enough I think that you do  figure out certain things about its  creator and and those were things I  tried to put into the book yeah that and  Edmond you also stressed the sense of of  universality and I wonder if it's it's a  virtual requirement of biography that  the subject have that kind of ranged  that you really are able to express  through your subject the same kinds of  Universal feelings that a painter would  or a composer well all men contain  multitudes I think it's quite possible  in fact it's often been proven that you  can write a biography of the highest  literary in scholarly quality about  somebody who was not Universal somebody  who might be indeed rather minor for  example aj simon's book the quest for  Corvo a book which is about the pursuit  of the recovery of an almost forgotten  minor figure in British literary history  and turned out to be one most  fascinating biographies ever written now  he was minor but AJ accurately Jair  Kohli wrote my dog tulip write wrote a  biography of his dog so and in Virginia  Woolf wrote a fictional biography of her  dog so I guess you can write about  anyone I have been thinking about since  our our amazingly enough is is nearly up  yeah I know we do talk on it's it's been  very entertaining I was thinking should  we have questions or should we what is  that sentence you quoted to me before  about when words when we've spoken  enough I was saying when people run out  of things to say they usually turn to  music because music is takes off where  words where words fail but  personally I think music would be  inappropriate at this time let me just  have a few questions you said it should  be spontaneous well all I will do is  illustrate a major theme of the book  which is that Beethoven as everybody  knows was deaf became deaf in his late  twenties began to become deaf and  suffered excruciating from tinnitus  through his thirties until he became so  deaf that the only way he could  communicate with him was to write  questions on paper now the confession  that he had tinnitus made me examine his  music to see if perhaps the pathology of  tinnitus the chiming the roarings the  buzzing in his ears might have somehow  come out to produce these very weird  sound effects you do get occasionally in  Beethoven frequently in Beethoven and I  talked about this to the American  Association of Neurological Surgeons in  New Orleans a couple of years ago and  happened to mention this idea of mine  and one of the neurologists got up and  said mr. Morris I have myself began to  lose my hearing and I'm studying the  phenomenon of deafness and he said I can  only tell you that the opening bars of  the Ninth Symphony led long sustain'd  hovering v that throbbing is exactly  what you hear when you're beginning to  lose your hearing so if you insist I  could demonstrate at least one passage  in there  it's from the rich Richie to teve at the  beginning of the slow movement of  Beethoven's piano sonata you know a flat  major opus 110  that a that reiterated a shrieks in his  music he uses do I think 75 times at the  finale of the Ninth Symphony I have no  proof of her but I'm quite sure that  derived from his tinnitus another  composer Friedrich Smetana actually  wrote a string quartet he also went deaf  in his late years in which in the last  movement it's suddenly interrupted by  this penetrating shrill high E which was  directly related to the sounds in his  head so it's a theory and what the hell  thank you all very much  wrapping up the aut